data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"input_json_delta","partial_json":"{\"kind\": \"decision\", \"title\": \"Store carts in Redis or Postgres?\", \"body\": \"Carts outgrow the 4 KB session cookie and get truncated. Moving cart state server-side fixes that; the cookie would hold only a session or cart ID.\\n\\n- **Redis:** fast, built-in TTL for abandoned carts, low per-request cost. Downside: a new service to run, and carts are lost on eviction or restart unless persistence is configured.\\n- **Postgres:** durable, queryable for abandoned-cart emails and analytics, and no new infrastructure if you already run it. Downside: a little more latency per cart update, plus a cleanup job for expired carts.\\n\\nRecommendation: Postgres, unless cart write volume is very high or you already operate Redis for something else.\", \"actions\": [{\"kind\":\"resolve\",\"label\":\"Postgres (Recommended)\",\"recommended\":true},{\"kind\":\"resolve\",\"label\":\"Redis\"}]}"}}

