data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":0,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"Redis and Postgres both fix the cookie limit by moving the cart server-side and leaving only an ID in the cookie. Redis is fast and handles expiry natively, which suits anonymous, disposable carts, but it adds a service to run and can lose data on restart or eviction unless persistence is configured. Postgres is durable, needs no new infrastructure if you already run it, and lets you query carts for abandoned-cart emails and analytics, at the cost of slightly slower writes and a cleanup job for expired carts. I recommend Postgres, unless cart write volume is very high or you already operate Redis.\n\nThe decision is recorded as ask A1 with Postgres (recommended) and Redis as options. I did not read or write any files."}}

